  revisionSummary: "UK payments regulation splits between the FCA (conduct) and the PRA (prudential soundness, part of the Bank of England), implemented in practice through the Payment Services Regulations (PSD2-style rules on authorisation, Strong Customer Authentication, complaints, and liability), alongside industry standards like PCI DSS and evolving APP fraud reimbursement rules. Emerging payment technologies span global instant payment schemes (UPI, Pix, FedNow/RTP), Request to Pay, Buy Now Pay Later (drawing growing regulatory scrutiny), and embedded finance delivered via APIs and Banking-as-a-Service. Digital currencies range from cryptocurrencies (decentralised, often volatile) to stablecoins (pegged, reserve-backed) to CBDCs (centrally issued, risk-free), with central banks including the Bank of England actively exploring a potential digital pound. Payments Operations in practice means monitoring exception queues, resolving issues within SLAs, and escalating appropriately, measured through KPIs like STP rate. And the full CPCM syllabus opens onto genuinely varied career pathways — Payments Operations, Treasury, Transaction Banking, Compliance/Financial Crime, and Product/Innovation — all resting on the same foundational knowledge this course has built from lesson one onward. This completes all 40 lessons of the CPCM curriculum.",
